# Storefront development environment — the values a local Docker Compose stack needs.
# Copy to storefront/.env before running `pnpm --filter storefront run dev`:
#
#     cp storefront/.env.example storefront/.env
#
# The two backend addresses below are REQUIRED and are no longer defaulted anywhere.
# Until 2026-09-06 twenty-four sites in this storefront answered an unset one with
# `http://localhost:3001`, and `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL` is a *browser* value that Next
# bakes into the bundle at build time — so a storefront built without it shipped a bundle
# pointing every visitor at their own machine, and the build reported success. Under D-195
# this tree is what every client instance is copied from, so that default reached every
# shop. `storefront/lib/env.ts` now refuses instead, naming the variable and this file.

# PORT — Next.js dev/start port. Auto-loaded by Next from .env / .env.local.
PORT=3000

# REQUIRED, read at RUN time by the process that serves the storefront: the server-side
# fetchers in lib/api/*. Host only, no /api/v1. In production this is the internal address
# (deploy/compose.prod.yml sets `http://backend:3001`), never the public one.
BACKEND_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001
# REQUIRED, inlined into the BROWSER bundle at BUILD time — a container cannot supply it
# later, so it has to be right when `next build` runs (storefront/Dockerfile takes it as a
# build arg). It must be the backend's *public* origin, reachable from a visitor's browser.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001
# REQUIRED, and inlined at BUILD time like the line above: the public origin this shop is
# served at. Every canonical link, the sitemap and robots.txt are built from it. Measured
# on this tree: after `next build` the variable's *name* is gone from `.next/server` and
# only its value remains, and `/robots.txt` is prerendered — its `Sitemap:` line is a
# static file inside the image. So setting this on a running container changes nothing;
# it has to be right when `next build` runs (storefront/Dockerfile takes it as a build
# arg, `.gitlab-ci.yml` passes `https://${STOREFRONT_DOMAIN}`). Until 2026-09-06 nothing
# in the deployment path set it at all, so every image this repository built served
# canonicals, a sitemap and a robots.txt naming the `http://localhost:3000` that
# `lib/seo/site-url.ts` falls back to — silently, and in the shop's own SEO surface.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000
NEXT_PUBLIC_SALES_CHANNEL_CODE=pl_default
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEFAULT_LOCALE=en-US
REVALIDATE_SECRET=change-me-shared-with-backend
